[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m [response] To solve this problem, we need to use the formula for the angular size of an object and the magnification formula for telescopes.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m 1. **Angular Diameter Calculation**:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    The angular diameter of the planet is given as \(45''\) (arcseconds). We need to convert this to radians because the angular size formula uses radians. There are \(3600''\) in a degree and \(180\) degrees in a radian, so:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    45'' = 45 \times \frac{1}{3600} \text{ degrees} = \frac{45}{3600} \text{ degrees} = \frac{45}{3600} \times \frac{\pi}{180} \text{ radians} = \frac{45 \pi}{648000} \text{ radians} = \frac{\pi}{14400} \text{ radians}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    The angular diameter \(\theta\) of the planet is \(\frac{\pi}{14400}\) radians.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m 2. **Linear Diameter Calculation**:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    The linear diameter \(D\) of the planet can be calculated using the formula:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    D = \theta \times f
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    where \(f\) is the focal length of the telescope. Here, \(f = 72 \text{ m}\). So,
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    D = \frac{\pi}{14400} \times 72 = \frac{72\pi}{14400} = \frac{\pi}{200} \text{ m}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    Converting this to centimeters:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    D = \frac{\pi}{200} \times 100 = \frac{100\pi}{200} = \frac{\pi}{2} \text{ cm} \approx 1.57 \text{ cm}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m 3. **Magnification Calculation**:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m    The magnification \(M\) of the telescope is given by the ratio of the focal length of the telescope to the focal length of the eyepiece. Since the problem does not specify the focal length of the eyepiece, we assume the telescope is used with an eyepiece of negligible focal length (i.e., the magnification is essentially the ratio of the focal lengths of the two telescopes). However, since both telescopes have the same focal length, the magnification is 1. Therefore, the diameter of the image of the planet is the same as the linear diameter of the planet.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4019687)[0m So, the diameter of the image of the planet is \(\frac{\pi}{2} \text{ cm}\).
